diff --git a/docs/architecture/updates.md b/docs/architecture/updates.md
index 69fd24dec..28558ac24 100644
--- a/docs/architecture/updates.md
+++ b/docs/architecture/updates.md
@@ -89,6 +89,7 @@ Linux cannot tell a distribution's package from a downloaded one, so its package
 
 - Play checks support the immediate flow only, preserve user cancellation, and resume a consented flow when its activity returns. Standalone runtime builds include the checksum-pinned Play In-App Updates SDK; embedding hosts supply it and retain its reflected entry points ([embedding](../public/en/embedding.md#android)).
 - The direct channel reads `releases/latest/download/` by default, so every release must carry the appcast of each target it updates: a target a release leaves out answers 404 until one carries it again.
+- Direct Android, macOS and Linux builds can embed ordered HTTPS mirror prefixes. The appcast reader tries the origin last, sharing the check deadline across attempts. Android and Linux stage each download separately and verify it before accepting the source. On macOS, Sparkle's delegate uses a loopback relay that serves the original appcast bytes and the selected same-origin package. After a partial transfer the relay requires a matching byte range from the next source. Sparkle verifies the complete package and handles installation.
 - WinSparkle saves `updates.automatic` as the user's choice on the first start, so a later change in `nsgo.yaml` reaches only new installs.
 - Linux asks `OnQuitRequest` before restarting after its package manager finishes. Cancellation keeps the current process running; a deferred answer can finish saving first. The package is already installed on disk. On Windows, WinSparkle waits on its worker thread for the same decision, leaving the app's main thread free; denial prevents installer launch, and cleanup releases an unanswered callback before joining the updater's workers. The approved shutdown bypasses a second close request, so a tray or window-close handler cannot strand an installer beside the old process.
 - The Linux appcast lists signed items for the release's Debian package (`.deb`), Arch package (`.pkg.tar.gz`) and AppImage (`.AppImage`). The Linux backend selects the matching item—installing `.deb` through `pkexec dpkg`, `.pkg.tar.gz`/`.pkg.tar.zst` through `pkexec pacman`, or atomically replacing `$APPIMAGE` with executable permissions before restarting—and falls back to the release's page for installs outside those formats; the backend resolves ownership once at launch, before any appcast request. Android hands its APK to the system installer, which asks the user and keeps store installs out of the direct channel.
diff --git a/docs/public/en/building.md b/docs/public/en/building.md
index 2e73f25f5..d3f8f968c 100644
--- a/docs/public/en/building.md
+++ b/docs/public/en/building.md
@@ -463,12 +463,15 @@ updates:
   key: zE0hx2FZwwHy6X7LbEP3GjTTE3KWfCAp5gsaBysC+vg=  # the public key nsgo keygen prints
   # channel: store                    # direct, store, steam or none; direct by default with a key, none without
   # feed: https://example.com/hello/  # where the appcasts and installers are; by default the latest GitHub release
+  # mirrors: [https://proxy.example.com/] # HTTPS URL prefixes, tried before the origin
   # automatic: false                  # check only when asked; true by default
   # automatic_downloads: true          # Sparkle downloads automatically; false by default, macOS only
 ```
 
 `nsgo keygen` prints a key pair and writes no file: put the public key in `updates.key` and commit it, and keep the secret one out of the repository, in a password manager and in `NSGO_UPDATES_KEY` where releases build, such as a GitHub Actions secret. Each direct build signs the installers with the secret key and writes `appcast-<target>.xml` beside them, a feed in [Sparkle](https://sparkle-project.org)'s format that the app reads under `feed`: upload the appcasts with the installers. Without the secret key, `build` writes no macOS or Windows appcast, as those install only signed installers, and Linux and Android apps reading an unsigned appcast open the release's page instead of installing its package.
 
+On Android, macOS and Linux direct builds with an HTTPS feed, `updates.mirrors` lists reverse proxies in priority order. Each proxy URL is the prefix, including its trailing slash, followed by the full original HTTPS URL. Appcasts and packages hosted on the same origin use this order, with the original URL last. The proxies serve update metadata and packages, and the updater still verifies package signatures before installing. On macOS Sparkle reads through a loopback relay; to resume an interrupted transfer the next source must support byte ranges.
+
 `-release-notes notes.md` puts a Markdown file of the release's notes, such as its changelog section, into each appcast, rendered as HTML without the file's own raw HTML. Sparkle and WinSparkle show it in their update dialogs, and the prompt on Linux and Android shows it as text below the version, up to 1,500 characters; apps read it as `core.UpdateCheck.Notes`.
 
 | Target | How a direct build updates |
